{
  "findings": [
    {
      "id": "read-error-and-buffered-tokens",
      "text": "Start a fresh default scanner whose first Read writes \"a\\r\\nb\" and returns the valid byte count with a non-EOF error E. The scanner adds the bytes to its buffer before recording E, then calls ScanLines with atEOF=true. The first Scan returns true with \"a\", the second returns true with \"b\", and the third returns false, with no further Read calls. Err returns the original E even while Scan is returning tokens. If the same Read returns io.EOF instead, the token sequence is identical and Err returns nil. If the count is impossible, count validation runs before either adding bytes or recording the supplied error, so Err reports ErrBadReadCount instead.",
      "citation": {
        "line": 224,
        "fragment": "s.end += n"
      },
      "question": "OPEN: Should the SplitFunc documentation explicitly say that atEOF is true after any recorded read failure?"
    },
    {
      "id": "final-token",
      "text": "With data buffered, a custom splitter that returns ErrFinalToken directly causes Scan to store the supplied token and mark the scanner done before validating or applying advance. A non-nil token, including a zero-length slice, makes that call return true. A nil token makes it return false. All later Scan calls return false without splitting or reading, and Bytes still exposes the stored value. Err returns nil if the prior error state was nil or io.EOF, and still returns any previously recorded non-EOF read error. A wrapped ErrFinalToken follows the ordinary split-error branch.",
      "citation": {
        "line": 152,
        "fragment": "if err == ErrFinalToken {"
      },
      "question": "OPEN: Should the documented stop \"with no error\" be scoped to the sentinel itself, since a previously recorded read error remains observable?"
    },
    {
      "id": "retry-after-split-failure",
      "text": "Start with buffered input and no recorded error. If a stateful splitter returns an ordinary error E on its first invocation, Scan records E and returns false before applying advance or assigning the returned token. If the caller invokes Scan again and the splitter now returns a valid positive advance with a non-nil token, it receives the same unprocessed bytes with atEOF=true, and Scan returns true with that token while Err still returns E. An invalid advance also returns false before consuming bytes or assigning a token. Such a failed call leaves any earlier token accessible until another path assigns it.",
      "citation": {
        "line": 150,
        "fragment": "s.split(s.buf[s.start:s.end], s.err != nil)"
      },
      "question": "OPEN: Does the contract intend a false return after a split failure to be terminal, given that a later Scan can return a token?"
    },
    {
      "id": "nonprogress-limits",
      "text": "With data buffered and no recorded error, each (0, non-nil token, nil) result returns true and resets the empty-token counter, even if the splitter repeats forever. If an error is already recorded, including io.EOF, the same result increments the counter, returns true through maxConsecutiveEmptyReads, and panics on the next such result. A positive-advance token resets the counter. Separately, if Scan needs input and the reader keeps returning (0, nil), the read loop records io.ErrNoProgress after more than maxConsecutiveEmptyReads consecutive attempts, then gives the splitter a chance to return tokens with atEOF=true.",
      "citation": {
        "line": 168,
        "fragment": "if s.err == nil || advance > 0 {"
      },
      "question": "OPEN: Should the documented panic condition specify that the token counter applies only after a recorded error and counts every non-nil token with zero advance, regardless of token length?"
    },
    {
      "id": "buffer-boundary-and-read-order",
      "text": "Give a fresh default scanner an initial buffer with capacity M>0 and max<=M. If its first Read fills all M bytes with a newline-free line and returns io.EOF with those bytes, ScanLines receives atEOF=true and Scan returns the M-byte token. If that Read instead returns (M, nil) and the reader would report EOF on its next call, ScanLines requests more input, but the full-buffer check returns false with ErrTooLong before that Read occurs. A subsequent Scan can then return the buffered line because ErrTooLong makes atEOF true, while Err continues to report ErrTooLong. As an ordinary control, a newline in the final buffer slot lets ScanLines return the line on the first Scan even when the Read returned no error.",
      "citation": {
        "line": 200,
        "fragment": "len(s.buf) >= s.maxTokenSize || len(s.buf) > maxInt/2"
      },
      "question": "OPEN: Should the documented strict size bound and unrecoverable stop describe the order in which the splitter sees EOF and the full-buffer check runs, including the token observable on a later Scan?"
    },
    {
      "id": "configuration-and-copying",
      "text": "Before the first Scan, Buffer and Split can be called repeatedly, with the last settings taking effect. A first Scan sets scanCalled before obtaining any token, so both setters panic afterward even if that Scan returned false. If a caller copies the Scanner value before first use, each copy begins with its own unset scanCalled field, and configuring one copy changes only that copy's fields. Both copies still hold the same reader value and, if a buffer was supplied before copying, slices backed by the same array. Copying after first use also copies scanCalled and the current scan state, so the setters panic on that copy too. For a shared stateful reader, reads through either scanner advance the same reader, while each scanner tracks its own buffered offsets.",
      "citation": {
        "line": 143,
        "fragment": "s.scanCalled = true"
      },
      "question": "OPEN: What copying and buffer ownership guarantees, if any, are intended before and after first use?"
    }
  ]
}
